%N A398694 Smallest 2-dense number (A174973) with n odd divisors.
%C A398694 In other words: a(n) is the smallest number with only one 2-dense sublist of divisors and n odd divisors.
%C A398694 a(n) is also the smallest number k whose symmetric representation of sigma(k) has only one part and n subparts.
%C A398694 Observation: a(A000040(k)) = A008776(A000040(k)-1) = A025192(A000040(k)), at least for 1 <= k <= 6.
%C A398694 From _Martin Ehrenstein_, Oct 16 2026: (Start)
%C A398694 a(A000040(k)) = A025192(A000040(k)) is true for all k.
%C A398694 Proof: To find the smallest even number with m odd divisors, simply find the smallest odd number with m divisors, and multiply by 2.
%C A398694 Consider the prime factorization of an odd number x = product(pk^ek) with ek>0. That number has m = tau(x) = A000005(x) = product(ek + 1) divisors. Given m the smallest x satisfying the aforementioned conditions needs to be found. For m prime, product(ek + 1) has exactly one multiplier > 1, thus k=1, and e_1 = m-1 and x = p_1^(m-1). The smallest x is obtained by choosing the smallest prime for p_1, and for odd numbers that is 3, thus x = 3^(m-1).
%C A398694 It remains to be shown that 2*x is 2-dense. The condition on prime factorizations from the second comment in A174973 is: 3 <= 2*(2^1) which is true.
%C A398694 Thus a(m) = 2*3^(m-1) for m prime. (End)
%H A398694 Martin Ehrenstein, <a href="/A398694/b398694.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..61</a>
%F A398694 a(p) = 2*3^(p-1) for primes p. - _Martin Ehrenstein_, Oct 16 2026
